A shoe store owner, with just one shoe store, who sells shoes and nothing but shoes, wants to design a DB schema. The owner is getting annoyed by having to search and check the shoes each and every time customer comes. Can you please design a shoe shore DB schema for the owner?
Specific requirements: brand, color and size.
There should be price and stock columns.
* Shoe owner should have a facility to give a discount.
* Customer comes and wants to buy multiple shoes at once. He should be able to do it.
* If a customer wants to return a shoe, I should be able to find that how much money I need to give back.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Shoe - id, modelName, brand, color, size, price, discountPercentage, stock,
Transactions - TransactionID, TransactionTimestamp, totalAmount,
TransactionDetails - TransactionDetailID, TransactionID, ShoeID, Quantity, unitPrice, discountPercentage, subTotalAmount,

Here, unitPrice and discountPercentage in TransactionDetails table are basically the price and discount of shoe at the time of transaction. Shoe table just have the price and discount of a shoe at any given point of time. We can have another table named ShoePriceHistory where we can create an entry for any change in price or discount of a shoe.
ShoePriceHistory - id, shoeId, timestamp, price, discountPercentage,

There can be multiple approaches to store the data of Shoe table:

Approach 1:
Shoe - id, modelName, brand, color, size, price, discountPercentage, stock,

HERE ACTUALLY DATA IS DENORMALIZED.

PROS - Simpler and requires fewer tables, More Intutive and Readable,
CONS - Redundancy, Maintenance, Consistency,
Redundancy - redundancy in data storage, as the same brand, color, and size information is repeated for each shoe
Maintenance - If you need to update a brand, color, or size, you would need to update multiple rows, leading to potential maintenance challenges.
Consistency - Ensuring consistency in data (e.g., making sure the same brand is spelled the same way across all rows) can be challenging.

This approach may be suitable for smaller data set.

Approach 2:

Shoe - id, modelNameId, brandId, colorId, sizeId, price, discountPercentage, stock,
ModelName - id, name, description, - index on name is good to have.
Brand - id, name, description, - index on name is good to have.
Color - id, name, description, - index on name is good to have.
Size - id, name, description, - index on name is good to have.

HERE DATA IS NORMALIZED.
PROS - improved data consistency and integrity as data is normalized, comparatively easier maintenance,
CONS - Schema more complex, multiple joins will be required to read the data, less intutive and difficult to read,

If data set is large, we should use this approach for easier maintenance and data consistency.

Approach 3:
Shoe - id, price, stock,
ShoeAttribute - id, name,
ShoeAttributeValue - id, shoeId, shoeAttributeId, value,

PROS - more scalable when there is a need to accommodate a variable set of attributes without altering the schema.
CONS - complex queries with multiple joins, no inherent control over which attributes are associated with a shoe and it relies heavily on application logic so enforcing data integrity and consistency becomes more challenging, not intutive and difficult to read, difficult to maintain,

Honestly, in place of using this approach in SQL, I will think of using NOSQL like MongoDB if I really need scalability for new attributes.

SO ACCORDING TO ME, BEST APPROACH IS APPROACH 2. INFACT WE SHOULD SEPARATE INVENTORY TABLE AS WELL TO MAKE THE DESIGN MORE SCALABLE. SO MY FINAL SCHEMA WILL BE:

